An enterprise-grade architectural blueprint designed to showcase production engineering standards: Zero-Trust EKS with Dynamic Multi-Tenant Karpenter Autoscaling.

Architecture Overview
 ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │ VPC (Private Subnets Only, VPC Endpoints / NAT Gateway)                │
 │                                                                        │
 │   EKS Control Plane (Private API Endpoint)                             │
 │      │                                                                 │
 │      ├── Core System Node Group (System-only workloads & Karpenter Pod)│
 │      │                                                                 │
 │      └── Karpenter Controller (IRSA / Pod Identity)                     │
 │             │                                                          │
 │             ▼ Directly calls AWS EC2 Fleet API                        │
 │   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │
 │   │ Karpenter Dynamic Data Plane (Provisioned on-demand)          │     │
 │   │                                                              │     │
 │   │   [NodePool: General API]    [NodePool: Batch Processing]    │     │
 │   │   - x86 Bottlerocket OS      - ARM64 Graviton3 / Spot        │     │
 │   │   - On-Demand / Nitro        - Consolidation enabled         │     │
 │   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │
 └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
Key Architectural Components
1. VPC & Networking Isolation
Zero Public Subnet Workloads: EKS Control Plane configured with private-only API endpoint access (accessible via Teleport/VPN).

Subnet Tagging Strategy: Auto-discovery configuration via Terraform tags:

karpenter.sh/discovery = var.cluster_name

kubernetes.io/role/internal-elb = 1

2. Security & Identity Blueprint
EKS Pod Identity / IRSA: Standard aws-auth ConfigMap is avoided in favor of EKS Pod Identity or strict IRSA. Karpenter runs under a highly restricted IAM role scoped to launching specific EC2 instances with designated tags.

Bottlerocket OS: Provision Karpenter EC2 Node Classes with AWS Bottlerocket instead of Amazon Linux. Bottlerocket features a read-only root filesystem, no shell/package manager, and minimal attack surface.

Node IAM Least-Privilege: Worker nodes provisioned by Karpenter receive standard AmazonEKSWorkerNodePolicy, AmazonEKS_CNI_Policy, and AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryReadOnly—with custom inline policies blocking access to IMDSv1 (http-tokens = required, http-put-response-hop-limit = 1).

3. Multi-NodePool Karpenter Engine
Instead of relying on rigid Auto Scaling Groups (ASGs), Karpenter is configured via EC2NodeClass and NodePool CRDs split by workload profile:

Critical Control Tier (System): Managed EKS Node Group running kube-system apps (CoreDNS, Metrics Server, Karpenter controller itself) across multi-AZ.

Tier A (Stateless Services): High-availability On-Demand instances, Graviton3 (c7g, m7g), spread across AZs via topology spread constraints.

Tier B (Batch Processing / Async Workers): Aggressive Spot instance fallback (c6i, c7g, m6i) with consolidationPolicy: WhenEmptyOrUnderutilized to minimize idle node spend.

