Getting this on terraform validate.

This error occurs because of a missing dollar sign ($) before {path.module} in modules/karpenter_resources/main.tf.

Without $, Terraform interprets {path.module} as a literal string folder named "{path.module}" instead of evaluating the variable path.

The Fix
Open modules/karpenter_resources/main.tf and update both kubectl_manifest resources to include the missing $ character.

❌ Before
Terraform
resource "kubectl_manifest" "ec2_node_class" {
  yaml_body = templatefile("{path.module}/manifests/ec2nodeclass.yaml", { # ❌ Missing $
    cluster_name   = var.cluster_name
    node_role_name = var.node_role_name
  })

  depends_on = [helm_release.karpenter]
}
✅ After
Terraform
resource "kubectl_manifest" "ec2_node_class" {
  yaml_body = templatefile("${path.module}/manifests/ec2nodeclass.yaml", { # ✅ Fixed: ${path.module}
    cluster_name   = var.cluster_name
    node_role_name = var.node_role_name
  })

  depends_on = [helm_release.karpenter]
}
Make sure your node_pool manifest resource right below it also uses ${path.module}:

Terraform
resource "kubectl_manifest" "node_pool" {
  yaml_body = templatefile("${path.module}/manifests/nodepool-stateless.yaml", { # ✅ Fixed: ${path.module}
    cpu_limit    = var.cpu_limit
    memory_limit = var.memory_limit
  })

  depends_on = [kubectl_manifest.ec2_node_class]
}
Verify the Fix
Re-run validation in your environment directory:

Bash
terraform validate
 Error: Invalid function argument

│ 

│   on ../../modules/karpenter_resources/main.tf line 36, in resource "kubectl_manifest" "node_pool":

│   36:   yaml_body = templatefile("${path.module}/manifests/nodepool-stateless.yaml", {

│   37:     cpu_limit    = var.cpu_limit

│   38:     memory_limit = var.memory_limit

│   39:   })

│     ├────────────────

│     │ while calling templatefile(path, vars)

│     │ path.module is "../../modules/karpenter_resources"

│ 

│ Invalid value for "path" parameter: no file exists at "../../modules/karpenter_resources/manifests/nodepool-stateless.yaml"; this function works only with

│ files that are distributed as part of the configuration source code, so if this file will be created by a resource in this configuration you must instead

│ obtain this result from an attribute of that resource.

╵



Still the same error.

This error means Terraform cannot locate the file nodepool-stateless.yaml at that relative directory path.

This usually happens if the file is named slightly differently, located in the wrong directory, or if the manifests/ folder sits under modules/karpenter_resources instead of inside it.

Step 1: Verify the File Location
Run this command from your root directory (terraform-eks-karpenter/):

Bash
ls -la modules/karpenter_resources/manifests/
Check for:

File extension mismatch: Is it .yml instead of .yaml?

Missing file: Did nodepool-stateless.yaml accidentally end up directly under modules/karpenter_resources/ instead of modules/karpenter_resources/manifests/?

Step 2: Correct Directory Placement
The manifest files must sit inside the child module's directory structure:

Plaintext
modules/
└── karpenter_resources/
    ├── main.tf
    ├── variables.tf
    └── manifests/                   <-- manifests subfolder inside module
        ├── ec2nodeclass.yaml
        └── nodepool-stateless.yaml  <-- file referenced by templatefile
If the manifests folder is currently located outside modules/karpenter_resources/, move it in:

Bash
# Move manifests inside the module if they were accidentally placed at root
mv manifests/ modules/karpenter_resources/
Step 3: Alternative Path Fix (If Files are Managed Outside Module)
If you prefer keeping your manifest files at the project root or environment level rather than inside modules/karpenter_resources/manifests/, pass the file path as a variable instead of hardcoding path.module.

Option A: Keeping files inside the module (Standard)
Ensure file exists at: modules/karpenter_resources/manifests/nodepool-stateless.yaml

Option B: Passing the file contents dynamically via variables
In modules/karpenter_resources/variables.tf:

Terraform
variable "node_pool_manifest_path" {
  type        = string
  default     = ""
  description = "Optional path override for NodePool template"
}
Then in modules/karpenter_resources/main.tf:

Terraform
resource "kubectl_manifest" "node_pool" {
  yaml_body = templatefile(
    var.node_pool_manifest_path != "" ? var.node_pool_manifest_path : "${path.module}/manifests/nodepool-stateless.yaml",
    {
      cpu_limit    = var.cpu_limit
      memory_limit = var.memory_limit
    }
  )

  depends_on = [kubectl_manifest.ec2_node_class]
}
Verify the Fix
Once the file is confirmed at modules/karpenter_resources/manifests/nodepool-stateless.yaml, re-run validation from environments/prod/:

Bash
terraform validate
ls -la modules/karpenter_resources/manifests/

total 16

drwxr-xr-x 2 taha taha 4096 Aug 26 17:17 .

drwxr-xr-x 3 taha taha 4096 Aug 30 16:49 ..

-rw-r--r-- 1 taha taha  638 Aug 26 17:53 ec2nodeclass.yaml

-rw-r--r-- 1 taha taha  837 Aug 30 16:18 nodepool_stateless.yaml

The file on your disk is named with an underscore: nodepool_stateless.yaml, but your Terraform code is looking for a hyphenated filename: nodepool-stateless.yaml.

Option 1: Rename the File (Recommended)
Rename the file on your disk to use a hyphen so it matches standard Kubernetes naming conventions and your Terraform configuration:

Bash
mv modules/karpenter_resources/manifests/nodepool_stateless.yaml modules/karpenter_resources/manifests/nodepool-stateless.yaml
Option 2: Update main.tf
Alternatively, update line 36 in modules/karpenter_resources/main.tf to use the underscore filename:

❌ Before
Terraform
resource "kubectl_manifest" "node_pool" {
  yaml_body = templatefile("${path.module}/manifests/nodepool-stateless.yaml", {
✅ After
Terraform
resource "kubectl_manifest" "node_pool" {
  yaml_body = templatefile("${path.module}/manifests/nodepool_stateless.yaml", {
Verify Validation
After applying either fix, run validation from your environment directory:

Bash
cd environments/prod
terraform validate
How many nodes would be launched by this cluster when it starts?



And what would be the size of the nodes?

When the cluster starts, it will launch 2 nodes initially.

Initial Node Count
System Node Group (Managed EKS): 2 nodes (desired_size = 2 in prod/terraform.tfvars).

Karpenter Data Plane: 0 nodes at startup. Karpenter is an event-driven autoscaler that provisions zero worker nodes by default. It only launches EC2 instances when pending pods (workloads) request resources that the system nodes cannot satisfy.

Node Specifications & Instance Sizes
1. Fixed System Nodes (Managed Node Group)
These host CoreDNS, Metrics Server, and the Karpenter controller pod itself.

Instance Type: m6i.large (2 vCPUs, 8 GiB RAM).

Disk Size: 20 GiB gp3 (default EKS managed node volume size).

OS: Standard Amazon Linux 2 / EKS Optimized AMI.

2. Karpenter Dynamic Nodes (When Workloads Scale Up)
When your application pods start requesting resources, Karpenter dynamically provisions instances matching the criteria set in nodepool-stateless.yaml and ec2nodeclass.yaml:

Instance Families: Any instance from the C (Compute), M (General Purpose), or R (Memory) families (e.g., c6i.xlarge, m7g.large, r6i.2xlarge).

Architectures: Automatically chooses between x86 (amd64) or ARM64 (arm64 Graviton) depending on pod architecture constraints and lowest cost.

Capacity Type: Mix of Spot and On-Demand (Karpenter picks the cheapest available instance type across available Availability Zones).

Disk Size & OS: 20 GiB encrypted gp3 volumes running security-hardened Bottlerocket OS.

The karpenter_resources doesn't have output.tf?

That's correct—in the current setup, modules/karpenter_resources doesn't strictly require an outputs.tf because it sits at the "leaf" end of your dependency graph. It consumes outputs from upstream modules (EKS, IAM) and creates Kubernetes manifests directly, so no other Terraform module depends on its results.

However, adding an outputs.tf to modules/karpenter_resources is standard practice for clean module design—it lets you inspect created manifests and explicitly surface status flags to your root environment.

modules/karpenter_resources/outputs.tf
Create modules/karpenter_resources/outputs.tf with the following definition:

Terraform
output "ec2_node_class_name" {
  description = "The metadata name of the applied Karpenter EC2NodeClass CRD"
  value       = kubectl_manifest.ec2_node_class.name
}

output "node_pool_name" {
  description = "The metadata name of the applied Karpenter NodePool CRD"
  value       = kubectl_manifest.node_pool.name
}

output "karpenter_helm_release_status" {
  description = "Status of the deployed Karpenter Helm release"
  value       = helm_release.karpenter.status
}
Pass Through to Root (environments/prod/outputs.tf)
To expose these details at the root level when running terraform apply, add them to environments/prod/outputs.tf:

Terraform
output "karpenter_node_pool_name" {
  description = "Name of the Karpenter NodePool handling dynamic scaling"
  value       = module.karpenter_resources.node_pool_name
}

output "karpenter_helm_status" {
  description = "Helm deployment status for Karpenter"
  value       = module.karpenter_resources.karpenter_helm_release_status
}
Okay, this project would be launched in a private subnet how can I communicate with the cluster through kubectl?

Because your EKS control plane API endpoint has public access disabled (cluster_endpoint_public_access = false), standard kubectl commands from your local computer will fail to reach the cluster directly.

There are three standard enterprise patterns to access a private EKS cluster safely:

Option 1: AWS SSM Session Manager Bastion (Easiest & Cheapest)
You deploy a lightweight, cheap EC2 instance (bastion) into your VPC private subnet running the AWS Systems Manager (SSM) Agent. You use aws ssm start-session to tunnel your local kubectl requests through SSH/SSM directly to the private API server.

[ Your Laptop ] ──SSM Tunnel via AWS CLI──> [ AWS SSM Agent ] ──> [ Private EKS Endpoint ]
                                              (Private Subnet)
How to implement via Terraform:
Add a minimal EC2 instance in modules/vpc with the AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore IAM policy attached—no open inbound SSH ports required.

How to connect from your local machine:
Use the AWS CLI SSM plugin to port-forward the EKS private endpoint to your localhost:

Bash
# Start an SSM port forward to the private cluster API (port 443)
aws ssm start-session \
  --target i-0123456789example \
  --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost \
  --parameters '{"host":["YOUR_EKS_PRIVATE_ENDPOINT_IP"],"portNumber":["443"],"localPortNumber":["6443"]}'
Option 2: AWS Client VPN (Best User Experience for Teams)
Set up an AWS Client VPN Endpoint associated with your VPC's private subnets.

[ Developer Laptop ] ────── OpenVPN Client ──────> [ AWS Client VPN ] ──> [ EKS Private API ]
How it works:
Connect via an OpenVPN-compatible client (or AWS VPN Client) using Mutual TLS or AWS IAM Identity Center (SSO).

Once connected, your local machine receives a virtual IP inside your VPC route table.

Standard kubectl commands work natively without extra port-forwarding scripts:

Bash
aws eks update-kubeconfig --region us-east-1 --name prod-eks-karpenter
kubectl get pods -A
Option 3: Teleport / Boundary (Production Zero-Trust Architecture)
In high-security enterprise environments, tools like Gravitational Teleport or HashiCorp Boundary are deployed inside the cluster.

Developers authenticate against an Identity Provider (Okta, Azure AD, Keycloak).

Teleport acts as an identity-aware proxy inside the VPC, logging and auditing every kubectl command executed without requiring open network access or static VPN certificates.

Summary Recommendation
For quick testing / initial dev setup: Use Option 1 (AWS SSM Tunnel)—costs under $5/month and requires no additional infrastructure setup.

For full engineering teams: Implement Option 2 (AWS Client VPN) for seamless developer access across dev, staging, and prod.

What would the AWS Client VPN module cost?

An AWS Client VPN consists of two separate billing components plus data transfer fees:

1. Core Cost Breakdown
Component	Price (US Regions)	Billing Type
Endpoint Subnet Association	$0.10 / hour per subnet	Continuous (24/7 once associated)
Active Client Connection	$0.05 / hour per connected user	Pay-per-use (prorated by minute)
Data Transfer Out	Standard AWS DTO rates (~$0.09/GB)	Usage-based
2. Practical Monthly Scenarios
Scenario A: Single Availability Zone (Cost-Optimized)
You associate 1 subnet for baseline connectivity:

Fixed Endpoint Cost: 1 subnet × $0.10/hr × 730 hrs = ~$73.00/month

Active Connections: 2 developers connected 40 hrs/week (~160 hrs total/mo)

160 hrs × $0.05/hr = $8.00/month

Total Estimated Cost: ~$81.00 / month

Scenario B: High-Availability (Multi-AZ Setup)
You associate 2 subnets across two Availability Zones for high availability:

Fixed Endpoint Cost: 2 subnets × $0.10/hr × 730 hrs = ~$146.00/month

Active Connections: 5 developers connected 40 hrs/week (~800 hrs total/mo)

800 hrs × $0.05/hr = $40.00/month

Total Estimated Cost: ~$186.00 / month

3. Key Cost Factors to Keep in Mind
Subnet Associations: You are billed per associated subnet. If you associate 3 subnets across 3 AZs, the fixed infrastructure fee alone is ~$219/month regardless of user activity.

Always-On Connections: If engineers leave their VPN connections running 24/7 (730 hours/month), a single connection costs ~$36.50/month. Setting an idle timeout in the endpoint settings reduces unnecessary costs.

Split Tunneling: Enable Split Tunneling in your configuration so only traffic destined for the VPC subnets routes through the VPN. This prevents paying AWS Data Transfer Out rates for general internet traffic (like streaming or web browsing).

Summary Comparison
AWS SSM Bastion (Option 1): ~$3–$5/month (runs on a single t3.micro / t4g.micro EC2 instance).

AWS Client VPN (Option 2): ~$80–$180+/month depending on AZ redundancies and team usage.

what if I launch a t3.micro in the same VPC in the public subnet?

Launching a t3.micro EC2 instance in a public subnet inside the same VPC is a standard, cost-effective way to manage a private EKS cluster.

[ Your Laptop ] ──SSM Session / SSH──> [ t3.micro Bastion ] ──> [ Private EKS Endpoint ]
                                       (Public Subnet)         (Private Subnet)
Cost & Performance Impact
Cost: ~$7.50 / month (or $0.00 if eligible for the AWS Free Tier of 750 hours/month of t2.micro/t3.micro).

Performance: Sufficient for running kubectl, helm, and administrative scripts.

Two Access Patterns for a Public Bastion
Pattern 1: SSM Session Manager (Recommended — Zero Open Inbound Ports)
Even though the EC2 instance sits in a public subnet, you do not need an Elastic IP or open port 22. You connect using the AWS SSM Agent via IAM authentication.

Bash
# 1. Connect directly to the bastion shell
aws ssm start-session --target i-0123456789example

# 2. Or tunnel kubectl requests from your local machine through the bastion
aws ssm start-session \
  --target i-0123456789example \
  --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost \
  --parameters '{"host":["<EKS_PRIVATE_API_ENDPOINT>"],"portNumber":["443"],"localPortNumber":["6443"]}'
Pattern 2: Standard SSH Tunneling
You assign an Elastic IP to the instance and open port 22 in its Security Group (restricted to your home/office IP address).

Bash
# SSH SOCKS proxy tunnel to route kubectl commands
ssh -i key.pem -D 1080 ec2-user@<BASTION_PUBLIC_IP>
Required Configuration Changes for Private EKS
To allow the bastion to talk to your cluster, you must configure two settings:

VPC Subnet Route Tables: Ensure the public subnet has an Internet Gateway (0.0.0.0/0 -> igw-xxxx) attached so SSM or SSH can communicate externally.

EKS Security Group Rule: Add an inbound rule on the EKS Cluster Security Group allowing TCP port 443 from the Bastion Instance's Security Group.

Terraform Implementation Module
You can add this as a module (modules/bastion/main.tf):

Terraform
data "aws_ami" "amazon_linux_2023" {
  most_recent = true
  owners      = ["amazon"]

  filter {
    name   = "name"
    values = ["al2023-ami-2023.*-x86_64"]
  }
}

resource "aws_security_group" "bastion_sg" {
  name        = "${var.cluster_name}-bastion-sg"
  description = "Security group for EKS Bastion host"
  vpc_id      = var.vpc_id

  # Outbound HTTPS for SSM Agent & Kubernetes API
  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  tags = {
    Name = "${var.cluster_name}-bastion-sg"
  }
}

# IAM Role allowing SSM Session Manager access without SSH keys
resource "aws_iam_role" "bastion_role" {
  name = "${var.cluster_name}-bastion-role"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [{
      Action    = "sts:AssumeRole"
      Effect    = "Allow"
      Principal = { Service = "ec2.amazonaws.com" }
    }]
  })
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "ssm_policy" {
  role       = aws_iam_role.bastion_role.name
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore"
}

resource "aws_iam_instance_profile" "bastion_profile" {
  name = "${var.cluster_name}-bastion-profile"
  role = aws_iam_role.bastion_role.name
}

resource "aws_instance" "bastion" {
  ami                  = data.aws_ami.amazon_linux_2023.id
  instance_type        = "t3.micro"
  subnet_id            = var.public_subnet_id
  vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.bastion_sg.id]
  iam_instance_profile = aws_iam_instance_profile.bastion_profile.name

  user_data = <<-EOF
              #!/bin/bash
              dnf update -y
              dnf install -y kubectl awscli git
              EOF

  tags = {
    Name = "${var.cluster_name}-bastion"
  }
}

# Allow Bastion to communicate with private EKS Control Plane
resource "aws_security_group_rule" "allow_bastion_to_eks" {
  type                     = "ingress"
  from_port                = 443
  to_port                  = 443
  protocol                 = "tcp"
  security_group_id        = var.eks_cluster_security_group_id
  source_security_group_id = aws_security_group.bastion_sg.id
  description              = "Allow HTTPS traffic from Bastion Host"
}
Okay, how can I integrate the SSM into the terraform code so that once the cluster is launched it can simply give me the command that needs to be run to connect to the cluster?

To make this seamless, you can deploy a dedicated SSM Bastion module in your Terraform code and output the exact commands needed to establish an SSM tunnel and route kubectl through it.

Step 1: Create the Bastion Module (modules/bastion/)
Create a new directory modules/bastion/ and add the following three files:

modules/bastion/variables.tf
Terraform
variable "cluster_name" { type = string }
variable "vpc_id" { type = string }
variable "public_subnet_id" { type = string }
variable "eks_cluster_security_group_id" { type = string }
modules/bastion/main.tf
Terraform
# 1. Fetch latest Amazon Linux 2023 AMI
data "aws_ami" "amazon_linux_2023" {
  most_recent = true
  owners      = ["amazon"]

  filter {
    name   = "name"
    values = ["al2023-ami-2023.*-x86_64"]
  }
}

# 2. Bastion Security Group (No Inbound SSH Required)
resource "aws_security_group" "bastion_sg" {
  name        = "${var.cluster_name}-bastion-sg"
  description = "Security group for EKS SSM Bastion host"
  vpc_id      = var.vpc_id

  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  tags = {
    Name = "${var.cluster_name}-bastion-sg"
  }
}

# 3. Allow Bastion to speak to EKS API on Port 443
resource "aws_security_group_rule" "allow_bastion_to_eks" {
  type                     = "ingress"
  from_port                = 443
  to_port                  = 443
  protocol                 = "tcp"
  security_group_id        = var.eks_cluster_security_group_id
  source_security_group_id = aws_security_group.bastion_sg.id
  description              = "Allow HTTPS traffic from Bastion Host"
}

# 4. IAM Role for Systems Manager (SSM)
resource "aws_iam_role" "bastion_role" {
  name = "${var.cluster_name}-bastion-role"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [{
      Action    = "sts:AssumeRole"
      Effect    = "Allow"
      Principal = { Service = "ec2.amazonaws.com" }
    }]
  })
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "ssm_policy" {
  role       = aws_iam_role.bastion_role.name
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore"
}

resource "aws_iam_instance_profile" "bastion_profile" {
  name = "${var.cluster_name}-bastion-profile"
  role = aws_iam_role.bastion_role.name
}

# 5. Bastion Instance
resource "aws_instance" "bastion" {
  ami                  = data.aws_ami.amazon_linux_2023.id
  instance_type        = "t3.micro"
  subnet_id            = var.public_subnet_id
  vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.bastion_sg.id]
  iam_instance_profile = aws_iam_instance_profile.bastion_profile.name

  tags = {
    Name = "${var.cluster_name}-bastion"
  }
}
modules/bastion/outputs.tf
Terraform
output "instance_id" {
  value = aws_instance.bastion.id
}
Step 2: Update Module Dependencies
We need the public_subnets from modules/vpc and the cluster's primary security group ID from modules/eks_control_plane.

Update modules/vpc/outputs.tf:
Terraform
output "vpc_id" { value = module.vpc.vpc_id }
output "private_subnets" { value = module.vpc.private_subnets }
output "public_subnets" { value = module.vpc.public_subnets } # Added
Update modules/eks_control_plane/outputs.tf:
Terraform
output "cluster_name" { value = module.eks.cluster_name }
output "cluster_endpoint" { value = module.eks.cluster_endpoint }
output "cluster_certificate_authority_data" { value = module.eks.cluster_certificate_authority_data }
output "cluster_primary_security_group_id" { value = module.eks.cluster_primary_security_group_id } # Added
Step 3: Call the Bastion Module and Generate Connection Commands
Add the bastion module to environments/prod/main.tf:

Terraform
module "bastion" {
  source = "../../modules/bastion"

  cluster_name                  = module.eks.cluster_name
  vpc_id                        = module.vpc.vpc_id
  public_subnet_id              = module.vpc.public_subnets[0]
  eks_cluster_security_group_id = module.eks.cluster_primary_security_group_id
}
Now, create the automated connection string in environments/prod/outputs.tf:

Terraform
# Clean extract of host domain without "https://" prefix
locals {
  eks_endpoint_host = replace(module.eks.cluster_endpoint, "https://", "")
}

output "connect_step_1_start_ssm_tunnel" {
  description = "Run this command in Terminal 1 to start port-forwarding traffic to the private EKS API"
  value       = "aws ssm start-session --target ${module.bastion.instance_id} --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost --parameters '{\"host\":[\"${local.eks_endpoint_host}\"],\"portNumber\":[\"443\"],\"localPortNumber\":[\"8443\"]}'"
}

output "connect_step_2_setup_kubeconfig" {
  description = "Run this in Terminal 2 to point your local kubectl to the forwarded tunnel"
  value       = "aws eks update-kubeconfig --name ${module.eks.cluster_name} && kubectl config set-cluster arn:aws:eks:${var.aws_region}:$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text):cluster/${module.eks.cluster_name} --server=https://localhost:8443"
}
How You Connect After Running terraform apply
When Terraform completes, it will display the connection instructions directly on your screen:

Bash
Outputs:

connect_step_1_start_ssm_tunnel = "aws ssm start-session --target i-0abcd1234efgh5678 --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost --parameters '{"host":["1234567890ABCDEF.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com"],"portNumber":["443"],"localPortNumber":["8443"]}'"

connect_step_2_setup_kubeconfig = "aws eks update-kubeconfig --name prod-eks-karpenter && kubectl config set-cluster arn:aws:eks:us-east-1:123456789012:cluster/prod-eks-karpenter --server=https://localhost:8443"
Terminal 1: Copy-paste connect_step_1_start_ssm_tunnel and leave it running. This opens an SSM encrypted tunnel to your private cluster via port 8443.

Terminal 2: Copy-paste connect_step_2_setup_kubeconfig.

Verify: Run kubectl get pods -A from your local terminal. All traffic routes through the SSM tunnel into the private EKS cluster.

