Getting this on plan

This error occurs because source_security_group_id expects a String (the ID of the security group), but you passed the entire Resource Object (aws_security_group.bastion_sg).

Fix
In modules/bastion/main.tf, append .id to the reference on line 34:

Terraform
# modules/bastion/main.tf

resource "aws_security_group_rule" "allow_bastion_to_eks" {
  type                     = "ingress"
  from_port                = 443
  to_port                  = 443
  protocol                 = "tcp"
  security_group_id        = var.eks_cluster_security_group_id
  source_security_group_id = aws_security_group.bastion_sg.id # <-- Add .id here
  description              = "Allow HTTPS traffic from Bastion Host"
}
Once updated, run terraform plan again and the error will be resolved.

Is it possible that if I launch eks with a kubernetes version that is not supported by aws eks it stucks in the launch state or it throws error?

It will throw an immediate validation error during creation (or fail fast during terraform plan / aws eks create-cluster), rather than getting stuck indefinitely in a launching/creating state.

1. What Happens at Creation Time
When you request an EKS cluster with an unsupported Kubernetes version:

Terraform (terraform plan / apply): AWS API returns an InvalidParameterException or ClientException almost immediately:

Error: creating EKS Cluster: InvalidParameterException: Unsupported Kubernetes version

AWS Console / CLI: The API refuses to accept the payload, and cluster creation is never initiated.

2. Why EKS Clusters Get Stuck in CREATING (Real Causes)
If your EKS cluster ever gets stuck in the CREATING state (often timing out after 20–30 minutes), it is almost always caused by network/VPC misconfigurations, not the Kubernetes version:

Missing Subnet Tags / Route Issues: The EKS control plane network interfaces (ENIs) cannot establish communication within your VPC subnets.

Insufficient Available IPs: Your target subnets do not have enough free IP addresses (EKS requires at least 6 free IPs per subnet for cross-account elastic network interfaces).

IAM Role Issues: The IAM role assigned to the EKS cluster (cluster_service_role) is missing the required AWS-managed policies (AmazonEKSClusterPolicy).

3. EKS Version Support Window (2026 Reference)
AWS EKS supports standard versions for 14 months, followed by Extended Support for up to 26 months:

Kubernetes Version	EKS Support Status
v1.28 – v1.32+	Fully Supported / Extended Support
v1.23 – v1.27	End of Life (Creation requests rejected by AWS API)
Summary: You don't need to worry about an invalid version causing an infinite hang. If the version is obsolete, AWS will fail fast and report the error instantly.

It failed with an error unfortunately I don't have the error is there a way I can get the error?



I have run terraform destroy now.

Even after running terraform destroy, you can still retrieve the exact error message and execution history through a few different methods:

1. Check the Local Terraform Crash/Log File
If Terraform encountered an unhandled panic or crashed, it writes a detailed crash log directly to your current directory:

Bash
# Search for terraform crash logs in your current working directory
ls -la crash.log crash-*.log 2>/dev/null
If present, view the last few lines:

Bash
cat crash.log | tail -n 50
2. Check AWS CloudTrail Events
Every call Terraform makes to AWS API endpoints (like CreateCluster or CreateNodegroup) is permanently logged in AWS CloudTrail, even if local state or resources are deleted.

Run this AWS CLI command to view recent API errors for EKS:

Bash
aws cloudtrail lookup-events \
  --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventSource,AttributeValue=eks.amazonaws.com \
  --query 'Events[?ErrorCode!=`null`].[EventTime, EventName, ErrorCode, ErrorMessage]' \
  --output table
To see all failed API calls across your AWS account in the last hour:

Bash
aws cloudtrail lookup-events \
  --start-time $(date -u -d '1 hour ago' +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ) \
  --query 'Events[?ErrorCode!=`null`].[EventTime, EventName, ErrorCode, ErrorMessage]' \
  --output table
3. Capture Logs on Next Run (Terraform Debug Mode)
When you re-run terraform apply, enable verbose logging so any failures are captured to a dedicated log file:

Bash
# Enable trace-level logging to a file
TF_LOG=DEBUG TF_LOG_PATH="terraform-debug.log" terraform apply -auto-approve
If it fails again, inspect the file for errors:

Bash
grep -i "error" terraform-debug.log | tail -n 30
Here is the last 30 lines containing error keyword.

