The root cause of your deployment failure is in line 2:

Plaintext
InvalidParameterException: Requested AMI for this version 1.30 is not supported
Why This Happened
When using Kubernetes v1.30+, AWS deprecated Amazon Linux 2 (AL2_x86_64) as the default operating system for managed node groups in favor of Amazon Linux 2023 (AL2023_x86_64_STANDARD).

If your node group configuration explicitly specifies ami_type = "AL2_x86_64" (or leaves it unset on an older module version that defaults to AL2_x86_64), AWS rejects the node group creation request with this exact error.

Step 1: Fix the ami_type in modules/eks_control_plane
Locate your EKS module configuration where system_nodes (or managed node groups) are defined, and set the AMI type to either AL2023_x86_64_STANDARD or BOTTLEROCKET_x86_64.

Option A: Upgrade to Amazon Linux 2023 (Recommended)
Terraform
eks_managed_node_groups = {
  system_nodes = {
    name           = "system-addons"
    instance_types = ["m6i.large"]
    
    # Update AMI type for Kubernetes 1.30+
    ami_type       = "AL2023_x86_64_STANDARD"
    
    min_size     = 2
    max_size     = 3
    desired_size = 2
  }
}
Option B: Use Bottlerocket OS
If you want your system node group aligned with Bottlerocket OS (matching Karpenter's dynamic nodes):

Terraform
eks_managed_node_groups = {
  system_nodes = {
    name           = "system-addons"
    instance_types = ["m6i.large"]
    
    ami_type       = "BOTTLEROCKET_x86_64"
    platform       = "bottlerocket"
    
    min_size     = 2
    max_size     = 3
    desired_size = 2
  }
}
Step 2: Clear Out Dated State and Apply
Since you already ran terraform destroy, purge any remaining local cache to ensure fresh parameters:

Bash
# Clean local cache state
rm -rf .terraform/ .terraform.lock.hcl

# Re-initialize and apply
terraform init
terraform apply
