Getting this error now.

This error occurs because aws_eks_pod_identity_association is attempting to make an AWS API call to associate an IAM role before the EKS Cluster control plane has finished creating.

Because Terraform evaluates resources in parallel without an explicit dependency link pointing directly from the Pod Identity module back to the EKS Cluster resource, it fires off CreatePodIdentityAssociation while the cluster is non-existent, triggering a 404 ResourceNotFoundException.  
Stack Overflow

The Fix: Add depends_on Dependency
Pass an explicit dependency on the EKS module inside the karpenter_iam module call in your root environments/prod/main.tf.

In environments/prod/main.tf
Locate your karpenter_iam module invocation and add depends_on = [module.eks]:

Terraform
module "karpenter_iam" {
  source = "terraform-aws-modules/eks-pod-identity/aws"
  version = "~> 1.0" # or your module reference

  name = "karpenter"
  
  # Pod identity configurations
  association_defaults = {
    namespace       = "karpenter"
    service_account = "karpenter"
  }

  attach_karpenter_controller_policy = true
  karpenter_controller_cluster_name  = module.eks.cluster_name
  karpenter_controller_node_iam_role_arns = [
    module.eks.eks_managed_node_groups["system_nodes"].iam_role_arn
  ]

  # ✅ FIX: Prevent race condition by forcing EKS Cluster creation first
  depends_on = [
    module.eks
  ]
}
Alternative: Passing cluster_name via Module Output
If your karpenter_iam module takes cluster_name directly, make sure it relies on the output attribute module.eks.cluster_name rather than a variable string like var.cluster_name. This forces Terraform's implicit graph solver to wait for the EKS module output.

Terraform
# ❌ Avoid: Using var directly breaks implicit dependency ordering
# cluster_name = var.cluster_name 

# ✅ Recommended: Using module output creates an implicit dependency graph link
cluster_name = module.eks.cluster_name
Re-applying the Infrastructure
Run standard target-based execution or re-run apply:

Bash
terraform apply
