This error (dial tcp 10.100.15.29:443: i/o timeout) occurs because your local Terraform client is attempting to deploy the Karpenter Helm chart by contacting the EKS API endpoint directly over the internet.

Because your cluster API endpoint is private-only (cluster_endpoint_public_access = false), your local machine cannot route directly to the cluster's private IP (10.100.15.29).

Why This Happens in Terraform
The helm and kubectl Terraform providers run locally on your machine during terraform apply. When they try to configure resources inside a private EKS cluster, the network request times out because standard internet routing cannot reach the VPC's private subnets.

Solution: Target-Based 2-Phase Apply
To resolve this cleanly without changing your Zero-Trust architecture, split the deployment into two distinct phases using Terraform targets:

Phase 1: Deploy Core AWS Infrastructure
Provision the VPC, EKS Cluster, System Nodes, IAM Roles, and SSM Bastion without calling the helm or kubectl providers.

Bash
terraform apply -target=module.vpc -target=module.eks -target=module.karpenter_iam -target=module.bastion
Phase 2: Establish SSM Tunnel & Deploy Kubernetes Resources
Once Phase 1 completes, Terraform will output the SSM tunnel command generated by your Bastion module.

Open Terminal 1 (Start SSM Tunnel):
Run the command surfaced in your Terraform outputs (connect_step_1_start_ssm_tunnel):

Bash
aws ssm start-session \
  --target i-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx \
  --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost \
  --parameters '{"host":["8E4C52F285A141F2B42D1175FE7C6BA1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com"],"portNumber":["443"],"localPortNumber":["8443"]}'
(Keep this terminal open and running)

Configure Local Environment:
In your working terminal, instruct your local Kubernetes context and Terraform providers to route traffic through localhost:8443:

Bash
aws eks update-kubeconfig --name prod-eks-karpenter
kubectl config set-cluster arn:aws:eks:us-east-1:123456789012:cluster/prod-eks-karpenter --server=https://localhost:8443
Open Terminal 2 (Complete Full Apply):
Ensure your helm / kubernetes provider configuration in providers.tf points to https://localhost:8443 (or uses standard kubeconfig context), then run:

Bash
terraform apply
Standard Helm Provider Setup for Private Clusters
Ensure your providers.tf file configures the Helm provider via the local proxy tunnel:

Terraform
provider "helm" {
  kubernetes {
    host                   = "https://localhost:8443"
    cluster_ca_certificate = base64decode(module.eks.cluster_certificate_authority_data)
    
    exec {
      api_version = "client.authentication.k8s.io/v1beta1"
      command     = "aws"
      args        = ["eks", "get-token", "--cluster-name", module.eks.cluster_name]
    }
  }
}
