Error parsing parameter '--parameters': Invalid JSON: Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes: line 1 column 2 (char 1)

JSON received: {\"host\":[\"8E4C52F285A141F2B42D1175FE7C6BA1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com\"],\"postNumber\":[\"443\"],\"localPortNumber\":[\"8443\"]}

The error occurs because your shell (Bash/Zsh) is passing the literal backslashes (\") into the AWS CLI, rendering the JSON string invalid. Additionally, there is a typo in the parameter name (postNumber instead of portNumber).

The Fix
Use single quotes around the JSON string without escaping the internal double quotes:

Bash
aws ssm start-session \
  --target i-0c2d72e33d271e1b5 \
  --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost \
  --parameters '{"host":["8E4C52F285A141F2B42D1175FE7C6BA1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com"],"portNumber":["443"],"localPortNumber":["8443"]}'
Fixing the Terraform Output Definition
To prevent Terraform from inserting escaped backslashes into your command outputs in the future, update the output block in environments/prod/outputs.tf to use jsonencode():

Terraform
output "connect_step_1_start_ssm_tunnel" {
  description = "Run this command in Terminal 1 to start port-forwarding"
  value       = "aws ssm start-session --target ${module.bastion.instance_id} --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost --parameters '${jsonencode({
    host            = [local.eks_endpoint_host]
    portNumber      = ["443"]
    localPortNumber = ["8443"]
  })}'"
}
Once the SSM session is established in Terminal 1, proceed with setting up your kubeconfig and running terraform apply in Terminal 2.

An error occurred (TargetNotConnected) when calling the StartSession operation: i-0c2d72e33d271e1b5 is not connected.

The TargetNotConnected error means AWS Systems Manager (SSM) cannot communicate with the agent inside your bastion EC2 instance. When an EC2 instance is first launched, it takes 1 to 3 minutes for the SSM agent to register online with the AWS Systems Manager control plane.

Run the following checks to diagnose and fix the connection:

Step 1: Check if the Bastion is Online in SSM
Run this AWS CLI command to see if AWS recognizes your instance as a managed node:

Bash
aws ssm describe-instance-information \
  --filters "Key=InstanceIds,Values=i-0c2d72e33d271e1b5" \
  --query "InstanceInformationList[*].[InstanceId,PingStatus,LastPingDateTime]" \
  --output table
If empty or PingStatus is ConnectionLost: The instance is still initializing, missing internet route access, or lacking proper IAM permissions.

If PingStatus is Online: Retry your aws ssm start-session command—it was likely just executed too quickly after creation.

Step 2: Common Root Causes & Infrastructure Fixes
If it stays offline after waiting 3 minutes, verify these three configuration requirements in your Terraform code:

1. Outbound Internet Access (Public Subnet Route Table)
The bastion instance needs outbound access to port 443 (HTTPS) to reach the AWS SSM service endpoints (ssm.us-east-1.amazonaws.com).  
Alexander Hose

Ensure the public subnet where the bastion is deployed has a route pointing to an Internet Gateway:

Terraform
# Route table for public subnet
route {
  cidr_block = "0.0.0.0/0"
  gateway_id = aws_internet_gateway.main.id
}
2. Security Group Outbound Traffic
Verify that aws_security_group.bastion_sg allows outbound HTTPS traffic (port 443 or all traffic -1) to 0.0.0.0/0:  
Alexander Hose

Terraform
resource "aws_security_group" "bastion_sg" {
  name   = "${var.cluster_name}-bastion-sg"
  vpc_id = var.vpc_id

  # ✅ Must allow outbound access so the SSM agent can reach AWS endpoints
  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }
}
3. IAM Instance Profile
Verify that the AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore policy is attached to your IAM role and that the aws_iam_instance_profile is assigned to the aws_instance.bastion resource.  
Alexander Hose

Step 3: Quick Terminal Retry
Once the describe-instance-information command returns Online, re-run the port-forwarding tunnel command:

Bash
aws ssm start-session \
  --target i-0c2d72e33d271e1b5 \
  --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost \
  --parameters '{"host":["8E4C52F285A141F2B42D1175FE7C6BA1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com"],"portNumber":["443"],"localPortNumber":["8443"]}'
