Where you place this configuration depends on how you built your VPC: using the official AWS VPC Terraform Module (terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws) or using custom Terraform resources (aws_vpc, aws_route_table, etc.).

Scenario A: You are using Custom Terraform Resources (modules/vpc/main.tf)
If you wrote custom resources for your VPC, create a dedicated public route table and associate it with your public subnets:  
Spacelift

Terraform
# 1. Route Table definition
resource "aws_route_table" "public" {
  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id

  route {
    cidr_block = "0.0.0.0/0"
    gateway_id = aws_internet_gateway.main.id
  }

  tags = {
    Name = "${var.cluster_name}-public-rt"
  }
}

# 2. Associate the Route Table with the Public Subnet where Bastion lives
resource "aws_route_table_association" "public" {
  count          = length(var.public_subnet_cidrs)
  subnet_id      = aws_subnet.public[count.index].id
  route_table_id = aws_route_table.public.id
}
Scenario B: You are using the Official AWS VPC Module (modules/vpc/main.tf)
If you are using terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws, do not write standard resource "aws_route_table" blocks. The module creates and configures the Internet Gateway and Public Route Tables automatically when public subnets are passed:

Terraform
module "vpc" {
  source  = "terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws"
  version = "~> 5.0"

  name = var.cluster_name
  cidr = "10.100.0.0/16"

  azs             = ["us-east-1a", "us-east-1b"]
  private_subnets = ["10.100.1.0/24", "10.100.2.0/24"]
  
  # ✅ Defining public_subnets automatically creates:
  # 1. An Internet Gateway (IGW)
  # 2. A Public Route Table with 0.0.0.0/0 -> IGW
  # 3. Route table associations for these subnets
  public_subnets  = ["10.100.101.0/24", "10.100.102.0/24"]

  enable_nat_gateway = true
  single_nat_gateway = true

  tags = {
    Environment = "prod"
  }
}
How to Verify Which One to Fix
If your Bastion is stuck at TargetNotConnected, check the following in AWS Console or CLI:

Check Subnet Placement: Ensure public_subnet_id = module.vpc.public_subnets[0] is passed into your bastion module (and not a private subnet ID).

Auto-Assign Public IP: Ensure the Bastion instance gets a public IP address:

Terraform
resource "aws_instance" "bastion" {
  # ...
  associate_public_ip_address = true  # Force public IP assignment
}
The bastion instance do have the IGW added to the public route table.

Since the public route table and Internet Gateway are already in place, three primary culprits cause TargetNotConnected:  
Alexander Hose

Auto-Assign Public IP: The EC2 instance sits in a public subnet, but if associate_public_ip_address = true is not explicitly enabled, it launched without a public IP address—meaning it cannot reach AWS Systems Manager endpoints over the internet.

Missing Outbound Security Group Rule: The instance's security group lacks an egress rule allowing outbound HTTPS (port 443) or all traffic (-1) to 0.0.0.0/0.  
Alexander Hose

Region Mismatch / AWS CLI Session: The local AWS CLI configuration profile points to a different region than where the bastion host lives (e.g., CLI default is us-west-2, but bastion is in us-east-1).  
GitHub

Step 1: Update modules/bastion/main.tf
Ensure associate_public_ip_address = true and the egress rule are present:

Terraform
# modules/bastion/main.tf

resource "aws_security_group" "bastion_sg" {
  name        = "${var.cluster_name}-bastion-sg"
  description = "Security group for EKS SSM Bastion host"
  vpc_id      = var.vpc_id

  # MUST allow outbound traffic to reach AWS SSM API endpoints over HTTPS
  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  tags = {
    Name = "${var.cluster_name}-bastion-sg"
  }
}

resource "aws_instance" "bastion" {
  ami                         = data.aws_ami.amazon_linux_2023.id
  instance_type               = "t3.micro"
  subnet_id                   = var.public_subnet_id
  vpc_security_group_ids      = [aws_security_group.bastion_sg.id]
  iam_instance_profile        = aws_iam_instance_profile.bastion_profile.name

  # ✅ ENSURE PUBLIC IP IS ASSIGNED
  associate_public_ip_address = true

  tags = {
    Name = "${var.cluster_name}-bastion"
  }
}
Step 2: Force-Recreate the Bastion Host
Apply the changes to force Terraform to re-provision the instance with a public IP:

Bash
terraform apply -target=module.bastion -auto-approve
