(You should see a message confirming the session started: Waiting for connections...)

Step 2: Update Local Kubeconfig & Complete Apply (Terminal 2)
In Terminal 2, direct your local kubectl configuration to talk to the local end of your SSM tunnel:

Bash
# 1. Generate standard kubeconfig
aws eks update-kubeconfig --region us-east-1 --name prod-eks-karpenter

# 2. Redirect cluster server URL to localhost:8443
kubectl config set-cluster arn:aws:eks:us-east-1:$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text):cluster/prod-eks-karpenter --server=https://localhost:8443

# 3. Test cluster connectivity over the tunnel
kubectl get nodes

# 4. Finish full Terraform deployment
terraform apply
Verification
If kubectl get nodes returns your system node pool and terraform apply completes without i/o timeout errors, your private EKS control plane deployment and Karpenter Helm releases are fully provisioned.

Is this correct for fixing the output definition?

  value       = "aws ssm start-session --target ${module.bastion.instance_id} --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost --parameters '${jsonencode({\"host\":[\"${local.eks_endpoint_host}\"],\"postNumber\":[\"443\"],\"localPortNumber\":[\"8443\"]})}'"

No, that line has two syntax/typo issues that will cause it to fail:

Typo in parameter key ("postNumber" instead of "portNumber"): AWS Systems Manager will reject this because the key name must be portNumber.

Double-escaped backslashes (\" inside HCL): In HCL, inside a jsonencode({ ... }) map, keys and values use standard HCL/JSON syntax. You do not need backslashes \" inside the object structure—adding them creates extra backslashes in the output string.

Correct HCL Output Block
Here is the clean, working configuration for environments/prod/outputs.tf:

Terraform
output "connect_step_1_start_ssm_tunnel" {
  description = "Run this command in Terminal 1 to start port-forwarding"
  value       = "aws ssm start-session --target ${module.bastion.instance_id} --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost --parameters '${jsonencode({
    host            = [local.eks_endpoint_host]
    portNumber      = ["443"]
    localPortNumber = ["8443"]
  })}'"
}
Why this works cleanly:
No escaping required: jsonencode() handles double quotes for JSON formatting automatically.

Clean shell execution: Wrapping the whole --parameters argument in single quotes '...' ensures your local terminal (Bash/Zsh) parses the JSON object properly without requiring manual string escaping.

SessionManagerPlugin is not found. Please refer to SessionManager Documentation here: http://docs.aws.amazon.com/console/systems-manager/session-manager-plugin-not-found

This error occurs because the AWS CLI relies on an external helper binary—the Session Manager plugin—to execute port-forwarding and interactive shell sessions over SSM.

To fix this, install the plugin on your local operating system.

Installation Instructions
macOS
Using Homebrew:

Bash
brew install --cask session-manager-plugin
Or manually via PKG:

Bash
curl "https://s3.amazonaws.com/session-manager-downloads/plugin/latest/mac/sessionmanager-bundle.zip" -o "sessionmanager-bundle.zip"
unzip sessionmanager-bundle.zip
sudo ./sessionmanager-bundle/install -i /usr/local/sessionmanagerplugin -b /usr/local/bin/session-manager-plugin
Linux (Ubuntu / Debian / AL2023 / RHEL)
Ubuntu / Debian (Ubuntu 22.04+ / Debian):

Bash
curl "https://s3.amazonaws.com/session-manager-downloads/plugin/latest/ubuntu_64bit/session-manager-plugin.deb" -o "session-manager-plugin.deb"
sudo dpkg -i session-manager-plugin.deb
Fedora / RHEL / CentOS:

Bash
curl "https://s3.amazonaws.com/session-manager-downloads/plugin/latest/linux_64bit/session-manager-plugin.rpm" -o "session-manager-plugin.rpm"
sudo yum install -y session-manager-plugin.rpm
Windows
Using PowerShell as Administrator:

PowerShell
Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "https://s3.amazonaws.com/session-manager-downloads/plugin/latest/windows/SessionManagerPluginSetup.exe" -OutFile "SessionManagerPluginSetup.exe"
Start-Process -FilePath ".\SessionManagerPluginSetup.exe" -ArgumentList "/S" -Wait
Step 2: Verify Installation
Run the following command in a new terminal window to verify it's correctly mapped in your PATH:

Bash
session-manager-plugin
(If successful, it will output: The Session Manager plugin was installed successfully.)

Step 3: Re-run Your SSM Tunnel
Once verified, run your SSM tunnel command again in Terminal 1:

Bash
aws ssm start-session \
  --region us-east-1 \
  --target i-0f935f451f27d0a59 \
  --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost \
  --parameters '{"host":["8E4C52F285A141F2B42D1175FE7C6BA1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com"],"portNumber":["443"],"localPortNumber":["8443"]}'
You should now see:

Plaintext
