E0831 23:11:00.261429   12582 memcache.go:265] "Unhandled Error" err="couldn't get current server API group list: Get \"https://localhost:8443/api?timeout=32s\": tls: failed to verify certificate: x509: certificate is valid for 8e4c52f285a141f2b42d1175fe7c6ba1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com, ip-172-16-174-152.ec2.internal, kubernetes, kubernetes.default, kubernetes.default.svc, kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local, not localhost"

E0831 23:11:00.393111   12582 memcache.go:265] "Unhandled Error" err="couldn't get current server API group list: Get \"https://localhost:8443/api?timeout=32s\": tls: failed to verify certificate: x509: certificate is valid for 8e4c52f285a141f2b42d1175fe7c6ba1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com, ip-172-16-174-152.ec2.internal, kubernetes, kubernetes.default, kubernetes.default.svc, kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local, not localhost"

Unable to connect to the server: tls: failed to verify certificate: x509: certificate is valid for 8e4c52f285a141f2b42d1175fe7c6ba1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com, ip-172-16-174-152.ec2.internal, kubernetes, kubernetes.default, kubernetes.default.svc, kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local, not localhost

This TLS verification error happens because the EKS API server's x509 certificate is issued specifically to its AWS domain name (8e4c52f285a141f2b42d1175fe7c6ba1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com), not localhost. When kubectl sends traffic to https://localhost:8443, the TLS handshake fails.

Here are the two ways to fix this cleanly:

Option 1: Point Domain Name to Localhost via /etc/hosts (Recommended)
Instead of telling kubectl to talk to localhost, map the actual EKS domain name to 127.0.0.1 locally. This allows TLS verification to pass without bypassing security.

Add an entry to your local /etc/hosts file:

Bash
echo "127.0.0.1 8e4c52f285a141f2b42d1175fe7c6ba1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
Re-generate your standard kubeconfig:

Bash
aws eks update-kubeconfig --region us-east-1 --name prod-eks-karpenter
Update the port to match your SSM tunnel (8443):

Bash
kubectl config set-cluster arn:aws:eks:us-east-1:993568083058:cluster/prod-eks-karpenter --server=https://8e4c52f285a141f2b42d1175fe7c6ba1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com:8443
Verify connection:

Bash
kubectl get nodes
Option 2: Bypass TLS Verification for Localhost (Quick Testing)
If you do not want to modify /etc/hosts, instruct kubectl to skip TLS verification for the local server endpoint:

Bash
kubectl config set-cluster arn:aws:eks:us-east-1:993568083058:cluster/prod-eks-karpenter --insecure-skip-tls-verify=true
Now try running kubectl get nodes or continuing with your terraform apply step.

kubectl get pods

E0831 23:13:19.248008   13051 memcache.go:265] "Unhandled Error" err="couldn't get current server API group list: Get \"https://8E4C52F285A141F2B42D1175FE7C6BA1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com/api?timeout=32s\": dial tcp 127.0.0.1:443: connect: connection refused"

E0831 23:13:19.249730   13051 memcache.go:265] "Unhandled Error" err="couldn't get current server API group list: Get \"https://8E4C52F285A141F2B42D1175FE7C6BA1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com/api?timeout=32s\": dial tcp 127.0.0.1:443: connect: connection refused"

E0831 23:13:19.251403   13051 memcache.go:265] "Unhandled Error" err="couldn't get current server API group list: Get \"https://8E4C52F285A141F2B42D1175FE7C6BA1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com/api?timeout=32s\": dial tcp 127.0.0.1:443: connect: connection refused"

E0831 23:13:19.253412   13051 memcache.go:265] "Unhandled Error" err="couldn't get current server API group list: Get \"https://8E4C52F285A141F2B42D1175FE7C6BA1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com/api?timeout=32s\": dial tcp 127.0.0.1:443: connect: connection refused"

E0831 23:13:19.255030   13051 memcache.go:265] "Unhandled Error" err="couldn't get current server API group list: Get \"https://8E4C52F285A141F2B42D1175FE7C6BA1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com/api?timeout=32s\": dial tcp 127.0.0.1:443: connect: connection refused"

The connection to the server 8E4C52F285A141F2B42D1175FE7C6BA1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com was refused - did you specify the right host or port?

The error shows kubectl is trying to connect to port 443 (127.0.0.1:443), but your SSM tunnel is listening on local port 8443.

When you ran aws eks update-kubeconfig, AWS reset the cluster URL back to default port 443.

The Fix
Append port :8443 to the server URL in your kubeconfig:

Bash
kubectl config set-cluster arn:aws:eks:us-east-1:993568083058:cluster/prod-eks-karpenter \
  --server=https://8E4C52F285A141F2B42D1175FE7C6BA1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com:8443
Verification
Make sure your SSM tunnel is still active in Terminal 1, then test the connection:

Bash
kubectl get nodes
Once that returns your node status successfully, you can proceed with terraform apply.

Okay, it seems to have worked now.

That is a huge win! Getting a fully private EKS cluster to talk through an SSM port-forwarding tunnel while maintaining strict TLS host verification can be tricky, but you’ve got a rock-solid Zero-Trust administrative path established now.

Now that the tunnel is verified and kubectl is responding, your environment is ready for the remaining infrastructure lifecycle.

