## 📁 Repository Structure

```text
.
├── environments/
│   └── prod/
│       ├── main.tf              # Root orchestrator for VPC, EKS, Bastion, & Karpenter
│       ├── variables.tf         # Production input variables
│       ├── outputs.tf           # Connection strings & SSM tunnel output commands
│       └── providers.tf         # AWS, Helm, and Kubernetes provider definitions
├── modules/
│   ├── vpc/                     # Multi-AZ VPC module with NAT & IGW
│   ├── eks_control_plane/       # EKS cluster, managed node groups, security groups
│   ├── bastion/                 # Private/Public SSM Bastion host setup
│   ├── karpenter_iam/           # IAM Roles and EKS Pod Identity Associations
│   └── karpenter_resources/     # Helm release & Karpenter NodePool / NodeClass manifests
├── README.md                    # Project documentation
└── track.log                    # Technical issue log & troubleshooting history
🚀 Deployment Guide
Prerequisites
AWS CLI v2 configured with appropriate administrator credentials.

Terraform (>= 1.5.0).

kubectl (v1.30+).

AWS Session Manager Plugin installed locally:

macOS: brew install --cask session-manager-plugin

Linux: sudo dpkg -i session-manager-plugin.deb or sudo yum install session-manager-plugin.rpm

Step 1: Deploy Core AWS Infrastructure (Phase 1)
Provision network, cluster control plane, bastion host, and IAM roles without attempting K8s API connections:

Bash
cd environments/prod
terraform init
terraform apply -target=module.vpc -target=module.eks -target=module.karpenter_iam -target=module.bastion
Step 2: Establish SSM Port-Forwarding Tunnel
Obtain the Bastion Instance ID and EKS Endpoint from Terraform outputs.

Map the EKS Endpoint domain locally in /etc/hosts to enable TLS verification over the tunnel:

Bash
# Add EKS endpoint host to local loopback
echo "127.0.0.1 <YOUR-EKS-CLUSTER-ENDPOINT-HOST>" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
Start the SSM tunnel in Terminal 1:

Bash
aws ssm start-session \
  --region us-east-1 \
  --target <BASTION_INSTANCE_ID> \
  --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost \
  --parameters '{"host":["<EKS_ENDPOINT_HOST>"],"portNumber":["443"],"localPortNumber":["8443"]}'
Step 3: Configure Kubeconfig & Verify Access
In Terminal 2, direct your local kubeconfig to route through the SSM local port (8443):

Bash
# Update kubeconfig
aws eks update-kubeconfig --region us-east-1 --name prod-eks-karpenter

# Re-point the cluster server endpoint to local port 8443
kubectl config set-cluster arn:aws:eks:us-east-1:<ACCOUNT_ID>:cluster/prod-eks-karpenter \
  --server=https://<EKS_ENDPOINT_HOST>:8443

# Test connectivity
kubectl get nodes
Step 4: Complete Deployment (Phase 2)
Deploy the remaining Kubernetes-native resources (Helm releases, Karpenter manifests) over the established tunnel:

Bash
terraform apply
🛠️ Verification & Operations
Check System Nodes:

Bash
kubectl get nodes -l workload.type=system
Check Karpenter Controller Status:

Bash
kubectl get pods -n karpenter
View Provisioners & NodeClasses:

Bash
kubectl get nodepools,nodeclasses
📝 Troubleshooting & Issue History
All infrastructure hurdles, API errors, network routing issues, and resolution strategies encountered during development are documented in detail inside track.log.
