track_log_content = """================================================================================
TROUBLESHOOTING & DEVELOPMENT TRACK LOG
Project: Zero-Trust EKS Cluster with Karpenter & SSM Bastion
ISSUE #1: Unsupported Kubernetes Version & EKS Launch Behavior
Symptoms / Question:
Inquiry regarding whether launching EKS with an unsupported Kubernetes version
causes cluster creation to get stuck in the CREATING state indefinitely.
Root Cause:
EKS cluster creation fails fast during API validation. Stuck CREATING states
are almost always caused by VPC network issues (e.g., lack of available subnet
IPs, missing route table entries, or missing service IAM roles).
Resolution:
Clarified AWS API validation behavior (returns immediate InvalidParameterException).

ISSUE #2: EKS Managed Node Group Creation Failure (AMI Version Incompatibility)
Error Log:
"creating EKS Node Group (prod-eks-karpenter:system-addons-xxxx): operation error
EKS: CreateNodegroup, StatusCode: 400, InvalidParameterException: Requested AMI for
this version 1.30 is not supported"
Root Cause:
Kubernetes v1.30+ deprecated Amazon Linux 2 (AL2_x86_64) for EKS managed node
groups. Unspecified ami_type defaulted to AL2_x86_64, which is rejected by AWS.
Resolution:
Explicitly configured ami_type = "AL2023_x86_64_STANDARD" in the system managed
node group block and fixed the module parameter syntax from instance_type (singular)
to instance_types (plural).

ISSUE #3: Pod Identity Association Race Condition (404 ResourceNotFoundException)
Error Log:
"creating AWS EKS Pod Identity Association: operation error EKS: CreatePodIdentityAssociation,
StatusCode: 404, ResourceNotFoundException: No cluster found for name: prod-eks-karpenter"
Root Cause:
Terraform attempted to provision aws_eks_pod_identity_association in parallel
before the EKS cluster control plane creation completed due to missing explicit
graph dependency.
Resolution:
Added explicit depends_on = [module.eks] inside the karpenter_iam module block and
passed module.eks.cluster_name to establish implicit graph ordering.

ISSUE #4: Private EKS Endpoint Network Timeout during Helm Apply
Error Log:
"Error: Kubernetes cluster unreachable: Get "https://8E4C52F285A141F2B42D1175FE7C6BA1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com/version":
dial tcp 10.100.15.29:443: i/o timeout"
Root Cause:
helm_release.karpenter executed locally on the operator machine, which cannot
directly access the private EKS cluster IP (10.100.15.29:443) when public API
access is disabled.
Resolution:
Architected a 2-Phase Apply methodology:
Phase 1: terraform apply -target=module.vpc -target=module.eks ...
Phase 2: Establish SSM Session Manager tunnel to Bastion, then complete full terraform apply.

ISSUE #5: SSM Start-Session JSON Escaping & Parameter Typo
Error Log:
"Error parsing parameter '--parameters': Invalid JSON: Expecting property name
enclosed in double quotes"
Root Cause:
Over-escaped quotes (\") passed from shell string outputs and typo in key name
(postNumber instead of portNumber).
Resolution:
Refactored Terraform output block to use jsonencode() wrapped in single quotes:
value = "aws ssm start-session ... --parameters '${jsonencode({ host = [...], portNumber = ["443"], localPortNumber = ["8443"] })}'"

ISSUE #6: SSM Bastion TargetNotConnected Error
Error Log:
"An error occurred (TargetNotConnected) when calling the StartSession operation:
i-0c2d72e33d271e1b5 is not connected."
Root Cause:
Bastion host lacked an auto-assigned public IP address in the public subnet,
preventing the local SSM agent from establishing outbound HTTPS (port 443) connection
to AWS Systems Manager endpoints.
Resolution:
Set associate_public_ip_address = true in modules/bastion/main.tf and verified
unbound egress (0.0.0.0/0) in the security group. Re-created bastion instance to acquire public IP.

ISSUE #7: Missing AWS CLI SessionManagerPlugin Binary
Error Log:
"SessionManagerPlugin is not found. Please refer to SessionManager Documentation..."
Root Cause:
AWS CLI start-session command relies on an external plugin binary that was not installed.
Resolution:
Installed session-manager-plugin locally via OS package manager.

ISSUE #8: Localhost TLS Certificate Verification Failure (x509 Mismatch)
Error Log:
"tls: failed to verify certificate: x509: certificate is valid for
8e4c52f285a141f2b42d1175fe7c6ba1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com ..., not localhost"
Root Cause:
Connecting directly to https://localhost:8443 breaks x509 SAN validation because
the EKS API certificate is generated for the AWS EKS FQDN.
Resolution:
Added host entry in /etc/hosts pointing the cluster FQDN to 127.0.0.1, then
updated kubeconfig endpoint to https://<EKS_FQDN>:8443.

ISSUE #9: Kubeconfig Port Override Fallback (Connection Refused on 443)
Error Log:
"The connection to the server 8E4C52F285A141F2B42D1175FE7C6BA1.gr7.us-east-1.eks.amazonaws.com
was refused - dial tcp 127.0.0.1:443: connect: connection refused"
Root Cause:
Running aws eks update-kubeconfig reset the cluster server URL back to standard
port 443, bypassing local port 8443 where SSM tunnel was listening.
Resolution:
Executed kubectl config set-cluster ... --server=https://<EKS_FQDN>:8443 to ensure
kubectl traffic targets the active SSM tunnel port.
"""

with open("README.md", "w") as f:
f.write(readme_content.strip())

with open("track.log", "w") as f:
f.write(track_log_content.strip())

print("Files generated successfully.")


